==================================================================
PEAK POSITIONS — model vs LCDM (does the acoustic scale drift?)
==================================================================
  peak  model l   LCDM l  drift dl   dl/l %
     1      221      220        +1    +0.45
     2      537      536        +1    +0.19
     3      814      813        +1    +0.12
     4     1128     1126        +2    +0.18
     5     1424     1422        +2    +0.14
     6     1728     1726        +2    +0.12
     7     2013     2010        +3    +0.15

  mean fractional drift dl/l = +0.19%
  -> peaks drift by a ~CONSTANT FRACTION: this is an ACOUSTIC-SCALE (theta_s)
     mismatch. The peaks are in the wrong PLACE, not the wrong height.
     theta_s ~ shifts all peaks by l -> n*pi/theta_s, so a fixed % drift
     = a fixed % theta_s error. That is FIXABLE by moving H0 (or the
     recombination via varying_me) to restore theta_s.

==================================================================
RESIDUAL SHAPE (200 < l < 2000)
==================================================================
  RMS fractional residual = 0.45%
  corr(residual, dD/dl)   = -0.66
  a large |corr| means the residual tracks the peak SLOPE = a horizontal
  (l) SHIFT of the peaks = theta_s misalignment (fixable), not a vertical
  height error (which would not correlate with the slope).

==================================================================
100*theta_s from CLASS (Planck pins it to 1.04109, ~0.03%)
==================================================================
  PRTOE (derived stack)      100*theta_s = 1.04047
  LCDM (Planck)              100*theta_s = 1.04194

  difference: -0.14%  (Planck's error on theta_s is ~0.03%)
  -> theta_s is off by MORE than Planck's precision: this is the high-l
     killer, and it is a background-parameter fix (H0/varying_me), not a
     shape failure. Refit those and the high-l chi2 should collapse.
